    content: "# Ladakh Acclimatization Guide\n\nLeh's altitude of 3,500m means every visitor needs to take altitude seriously. Acute Mountain Sickness (AMS) can ruin - or end - a Ladakh trip if ignored.\n\n## Day 1: Arrive & Rest\nOn arrival in Leh, do NOT exert yourself. Walk slowly, avoid stairs, hydrate aggressively (3-4L water). First night is the most critical - sleep is the best medicine.\n\n## Day 2: Light Local Sightseeing\nGentle sightseeing around Leh town (palace, Shanti Stupa, market) helps your body adjust. Avoid Khardung La or Pangong on day 2 - many travellers make this mistake.\n\n## Day 3+: Higher Altitude\nAfter 2 nights in Leh you can attempt Nubra (3,048m, via Khardung La 5,359m) or Pangong (4,350m).\n\n## Golden Rules\n- **Diamox** (acetazolamide) 125mg twice daily - consult your doctor\n- **No alcohol** for the first 48 hours\n- **Hydrate** with 3-4L water daily\n- **Carry** an oxygen canister (₹400 in Leh markets)\n- **Descend** immediately if symptoms worsen\n\n## Symptoms of AMS\n- Headache, nausea, dizziness\n- Loss of appetite\n- Sleep disturbance\n- Shortness of breath\n\n**Severe symptoms** (confusion, fluid in lungs) require immediate descent - do not wait.",
