    content: "# Kashmiri Cuisine: The Wazwan Feast\n\nKashmiri cuisine is among India's most distinctive - a meat-centric, saffron-laced tradition rooted in the valley's Persian and Central Asian heritage.\n\n## What is Wazwan?\n**Wazwan** is the grand ceremonial feast of Kashmir - a 36-course meal traditionally served at weddings, prepared overnight by a **waza** (master chef). Diners sit around a large **trammi** (platter) and eat with their hands.\n\n## Must-Try Dishes\n\n### Meat\n- **Rogan Josh** - lamb in a fiery red gravy (Kashmir's signature dish)\n- **Gushtaba** - meatballs in a creamy yogurt gravy\n- **Rista** - meatballs in red saffron gravy\n- **Tabak Maaz** - fried lamb ribs\n- **Daniwal Korma** - lamb with coriander\n\n### Vegetarian\n- **Dum Aloo** - baby potatoes in spiced yogurt gravy\n- **Chaman** - paneer in creamy tomato gravy\n- **Haakh** - collard greens\n\n### Breads & Rice\n- **Kahwa** - saffron green tea\n- **Sheer Chai** - pink salt tea\n- **Lahsoi / Girda** - local breads\n- **Mutton Pulao** - fragrant basmati\n\n## Where to Try\nIn Srinagar, **Ahdoos** and **Mughal Darbar** are legendary for authentic Wazwan. For street food, try **Lal Chowk's** shawarma stalls and **Kathi Kebab** at Maisuma.\n\nWazwan is more than food - it's a ritual of Kashmiri hospitality.",
